  "thumbnailAlt": "Personal Training Intake form beside a photo of experienced instructor watching anonymous African American female lifting dumbbell on blurred background of gym",
  "content": {
    "intro": "A personal training intake form is a form that collects a new client's fitness goals, training background, health screening answers, injuries and weekly availability so a trainer can write a safe first program before the first session. This template builds the PAR-Q readiness questions into the flow and asks how the client likes to be coached, so the first hour is spent training rather than filling in paperwork.",
    "audience": "Independent personal trainers, online coaches, PT studios and gym-based trainers taking on new one-to-one or small-group clients.",
    "useCases": [
      "Sending a link after a free consultation so the client arrives ready to train",
      "Onboarding online coaching clients who will train on their own",
      "Re-assessing existing clients every 12 weeks to update goals",
      "Gym floor trainers sharing a QR code with members who book a taster session"
    ],
    "tips": [
      "Use conditional logic to show the injury detail box only when a client answers Yes to any PAR-Q question.",
      "Let clients upload a progress photo or a past program so you can see where they are starting from.",
      "Send each intake to Google Sheets to keep a client roster with goals and available days side by side."
    ],
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "What should a personal training intake form include?",
        "answer": "Contact and emergency details, the client's main goal and a measurable target, current activity level and training experience, the PAR-Q health questions, injuries or conditions, medications that affect exercise, weekly availability, preferred training location and coaching style, and agreement to your terms and assumption of risk."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a PAR-Q and do I need one?",
        "answer": "The PAR-Q (Physical Activity Readiness Questionnaire) is a short set of yes/no questions that flags people who should get medical clearance before starting exercise. Many insurers and certifying bodies expect trainers to use one; check with yours."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should I do if a client answers Yes to a health question?",
        "answer": "Ask them to speak to a doctor and get clearance before starting a new program, and keep a note of it with their intake. Trainers should not diagnose or give medical advice."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can online coaching clients use this form?",
        "answer": "Yes. It works on any phone, and clients can upload photos or files and save their progress if they need to check details."
      }
    ]
  },
